# The built-in interface, in load order: a bare filename is ours, from `assets/ui`; a path is
# the reference's own file, off the player's patch chain. Entry 0, Fonts.xml, loads alone into
# every boot VM, from Startup on; the rest loads on entering the world, then third-party addons.
# The font objects thus exist before the login screen; the reference loads Fonts.xml with the
# rest of FrameXML.
# `inherits=`, `parent=` and a named `relativeTo=` resolve only against names declared above; a
# missing one only warns (the template is skipped, the parent falls back to the enclosing frame).
# A later Lua global or template replaces an earlier one; a named frame publishes its global
# only while the name is free.
# A stock `.xml` that does not `<Script file=>` its own `.lua` needs both rows, `.lua` first.
# LoadOnDemand addons have no row, as in the reference; they load off the chain on first use.

## Interface: 11200
## Title: benilla

Interface\FrameXML\Fonts.xml

# Second, not first as in the reference, because entry 0 must be Fonts.xml.
Interface\FrameXML\GlobalStrings.lua
# ══ A SERVER'S OWN FRAMEXML PRELUDE — optional, and `?` is what says so (see `addons.rs`) ══
# Vanilla 1.12 has neither file. Turtle WoW's `FrameXML.toc` opens with them, at lines 4 and 5,
# straight after `GlobalStrings.lua` — which is the slot they get here, because the files that
# need them are loaded from that same install and expect them already run.
#
# What they carry is not decoration. `Globals.lua` defines `explode`, `trim`, `wipe`, `sizeof` and
# the addon-message delimiters — 43 call sites across `PaperDollFrame.lua`, `WorldStateFrame.lua`
# and the guild-bank UI. `Overrides.lua` defines `CONTINENTS_LENGTH`, which `WorldMapFrame.lua`
# compares against on every continent-dropdown update. Absent, the player's own FrameXML raises a
# red script error on opening the character sheet and again on the world map — which is exactly
# how this was found.
#
# They are `?` rather than plain entries because a vanilla install has no such files, and there a
# missing entry must stay silent: this manifest is ours, so a non-optional line the chain does not
# hold is our bug, and that guard is worth keeping sharp.
?Interface\FrameXML\Globals.lua
?Interface\FrameXML\Overrides.lua
# ══ THE LOCALE ADJUSTMENTS — the reference's own, off the player's chain (1751, window 5) ══
# `Localize()` (which this file also CALLS at load, exactly as the reference's own two-part
# `<Script>` does) and `LocalizeFrames()`. Nothing of ours ever shipped either, and the gap was
# recorded in two places: `GameTime.xml` set `TwentyFourHourTime = 1` itself with a note saying the
# reference sets it in `LocalizeFrames`, and `HelpFrame.xml` names a whole family of strings that
# "`LocalizeFrames()`, which benilla does not ship, so it never fired here either" would have set.
#
# It is the reference's own slot (FrameXML.toc l.3, straight after Fonts.xml) and the order is the
# point: `Localize` overwrites `GlobalStrings.lua` keys, and GlobalStrings is loaded ahead of this
# whole manifest at VM setup (`ui_script/mod.rs`) — which is FrameXML.toc l.1, so the two land in
# the reference's own order. The file it patches is the PLAYER'S, so the locale is theirs by
# construction rather than ours to guess.
#
# `LocalizeFrames()` is called from `UIParent_OnEvent`'s VARIABLES_LOADED arm — the reference's own
# caller (UIParent.lua l.230-235), added to our UIParent.xml with this window.
Interface\FrameXML\Localization.xml
# `GetText` is a Lua global, not an engine binding: the client's `0x703bf0` calls it.
Interface\FrameXML\LocaleProperties.lua
Interface\FrameXML\BasicControls.xml
